q1 0.0039, q2 0.0063, q3 6.30, q4 7.88, r 1600\n13. DN25: q1 0.0079, q2 0.0126, q3 6.30, q4 7.88, r 800\n14. DN25: q1 0.0158, q2 0.0252, q3 6.30, q4 7.88, r 400\n15. DN25: q1 0.0252, q2 0.0403, q3 6.30, q4 7.88, r 250\n\nWait — should q1 0.0040 vs 0.004 (trailing zeros)? Keep as numbers: 0.002, 0.004, 0.0064, 0.0016, 0.0031, 0.0063, 0.01, 0.0025. JSON numbers: 0.0020 = 0.002. Fine.\n\nHmm, but the model naming for DN15/20 variants: for the \"15 and 20\" columns, the meters come in both DN15 and DN20 with same metrology. I'll model each variant with a nominal_diameter field that can be a string \"15 and 20\" or list. In matrix table rows, I'll use strings.\n\nFor model_level characteristics, since the values vary across models, the attributes q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio, nominal_diameter all vary. Per rule 4, emit model_level entries. But 15 models × 6 attributes is a lot of JSON. The example shows model_level with each attribute and values per model. I'll do it, but perhaps I could rely on matrix_tables for full detail and provide model_level for the key varying attributes. The rule says \"EXTRACT as matrix_tables[] AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level\". So I should do all three. That's verbose but correct.\n\nAlternatively, for characteristics.model_level I can include: nominal_diameter (string values), q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio. That's 6 entries × 15 values each. Plus matrix_tables with 2 tables. Let me consider merging into a single matrix_table \"Basic metrological data (flowrates)\" with 15 rows — that might be cleaner and preserves both source tables. Actually the schema says preserve multi-dim tables; I can have two entries, one per source table, each with 8 and 8 rows (last row of table 2 is empty \"-\", which I should drop since it's not a value).\n\nHmm, dropping the empty 8th column of Table 2: nominal diameter \"-\", all values \"-\". Yes, drop it.\n\nI'll create two matrix_tables entries: \"Table 1 — Basic metrological dat