ally there's subtlety: variants 5-7 are \"15 and 20\" and 9-11 \"20 and 25\". Let me define model ids:\n\n1. \"DN15, Q3=1.6 m³/h, R800\" — but model_id should be compact. I'll use:\n- \"DN15_R800\"\n- \"DN15_R400\"\n- \"DN15_R250\"\n- \"DN15_R1600_Q3=2.5\"\nHmm, DN15 appears with Q3=1.6 (R800/400/250) and Q3=2.5 (R1600). So id must disambiguate: include Q3.\n\nLet me use format: \"Q3=1.6_R800_DN15\", \"Q3=1.6_R400_DN15\", \"Q3=1.6_R250_DN15\", \"Q3=2.5_R1600_DN15\", \"Q3=2.5_R800_DN15/20\", \"Q3=2.5_R400_DN15/20\", \"Q3=2.5_R250_DN15/20\", \"Q3=4.0_R1600_DN20\", \"Q3=4.0_R800_DN20/25\", \"Q3=4.0_R400_DN20/25\", \"Q3=4.0_R250_DN20/25\", \"Q3=6.3_R1600_DN25\", \"Q3=6.3_R800_DN25\", \"Q3=6.3_R400_DN25\", \"Q3=6.3_R250_DN25\".\n\nThese are unique. Good.\n\nmodel_level characteristics:\n- nominal_diameter_mm: values per model: \"15\", \"15\", \"15\", \"15\", \"15 and 20\", ... Strings since some are dual. unit mm.\n- q1 (m³/h), q2 (m³/h), q3 (m³/h), q4 (m³/h), q3_q1_ratio (dimensionless).\n\nThat's 5-6 attribute entries with 15 values each. That's 90 value objects. Plus matrix_tables duplicating. It's verbose but per instructions (\"MOST IMPORTANT\" matrix detection) it's expected. Alternatively, since matrix_tables fully capture it, maybe model_level can be limited to the main varying attributes. The R137 example populated model_level with qmax, qt, qmin (3 of 7 columns) while matrix_tables had all. So partial model_level is acceptable. But to be safe and complete, I'll include q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio in model_level. Nominal diameter as string values in models' attributes{} instead? Hmm.\n\nOption: put attributes{} in each model entry: {nominal_diameter: \"15\", q1: ..., ...} and skip model_level? The schema for models includes attributes{}. The examples show models with only model_id. But schema definition: \"models[]: {model_id, label, attributes{}}\". Including attributes per model is schema-conformant. However the rules emphasize model_level. The R137 example explicit