l (null when none), unit_id: null, footnote_markers only when non-empty. Actually to be safe, I'll always include unit_symbol and unit_id; include footnote_markers only when present (none here). Hmm, for string values like \"Ultrasonic\", unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null.\n\nThat adds bulk for 15-value model_level arrays: each value object {model, value, unit_symbol?, unit_id?}. The R137 example model_level values: {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} — no unit_symbol/unit_id per value; units at attribute level. I'll follow: model_level attribute entries have unit_symbol at top; values are {model, value} (+ footnote_markers when needed). Good, matches example.\n\ntype_level: single values with unit fields as objects per rule 2.\n\nNow matrix_tables with rows keyed by model_id. Include column_units. Rows:\n\nTable 1 (8 rows):\n{\"model_id\": \"Q3=1.6_R800_DN15\", \"nominal_diameter\": \"15\", \"q1\": 0.002, \"q2\": 0.0032, \"q3\": 1.6, \"q4\": 2.0, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 800}\n{\"model_id\": \"Q3=1.6_R400_DN15\", \"nominal_diameter\": \"15\", \"q1\": 0.004, \"q2\": 0.0064, \"q3\": 1.6, \"q4\": 2.0, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 400}\n{\"model_id\": \"Q3=1.6_R250_DN15\", \"nominal_diameter\": \"15\", \"q1\": 0.0064, \"q2\": 0.0102, \"q3\": 1.6, \"q4\": 2.0, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 250}\n{\"model_id\": \"Q3=2.5_R1600_DN15\", \"nominal_diameter\": \"15\", \"q1\": 0.0016, \"q2\": 0.0025, \"q3\": 2.5, \"q4\": 3.13, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 1600}\n{\"model_id\": \"Q3=2.5_R800_DN15/20\", \"nominal_diameter\": \"15 and 20\", \"q1\": 0.0031, \"q2\": 0.005, \"q3\": 2.5, \"q4\": 3.13, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 800}\n{\"model_id\": \"Q3=2.5_R400_DN15/20\", \"nominal_diameter\": \"15 and 20\", \"q1\": 0.0063, \"q2\": 0.01, \"q3\": 2.5, \"q4\": 3.13, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 400}\n{\"model_id\": \"Q3=2.5_R250_DN15/20\", \"nominal_diameter\": \"15 and 20\", \"q1\": 0.01, \"q2\": 0.016, \"q3\": 2.5, \"q4\": 3.13, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 250}\n{\"model_id\": \"Q3=4.0_R1600_DN20\", \"nominal_diameter\": \"20\", \"q1\": 0.0025, \"q2\": 0.004, \"q3\": 4.0, \"q4\": 5.0, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 1600}\n\nTable 2 (7 rows):\n{\"model_id\": \"Q3=4.0_R800_DN20/25\", \"nominal_diameter\": \"20 and 25\", \"q1\": 0.005, \"q2\": 0.008, \"q3\": 4.0, \"q4\": 5.0, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 800}\n{\"model_id\": \"Q3=4.0_R400_DN20/25\", \"nominal_diameter\": \"20 and 25\", \"q1\": 0.01, \"q2\": 0.016, \"q3\": 4.0, \"q4\": 5.0, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 400}\n{\"model_id\": \"Q3=4.0_R250_DN20/25\", \"nominal_diameter\": \"20 and 25\", \"q1\": 0.016, \"q2\": 0.0256, \"q3\": 4.0, \"q4\": 5.0, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 250}\n{\"model_id\": \"Q3=6.3_R1600_DN25\", \"nominal_diameter\": \"25\", \"q1\": 0.0039, \"q2\": 0.0063, \"q3\": 6.3, \"q4\": 7.88, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 1600}\n{\"model_id\": \"Q3=6.3_R800_DN25\", \"nominal_diameter\": \"25\", \"q1\": 0.0079, \"q2\": 0.0126, \"q3\": 6.3, \"q4\": 7.88, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 800}\n{\"model_id\": \"Q3=6.3_R400_DN25\", \"nominal_diameter\": \"25\", \"q1\": 0.0158, \"q2\": 0.0252, \"q3\": 6.3, \"q4\": 7.88, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 400}\n{\"model_id\": \"Q3=6.3_R250_DN25\", \"nominal_diameter\": \"25\", \"q1\": 0.0252, \"q2\": 0.0403, \"q3\": 6.3, \"q4\": 7.88, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 250}\n\nmodel_level entries — 6 attributes × 15 values. Let me write them.\n\nnominal_diameter (unit mm):\nvalues in same order:\nDN15 ×4 \"15\"; \"15 and 20\" ×3; \"20\"; \"20 and 25\" ×3; \"25\" ×4.\n\nq1 (m³/h): 0.002, 0.004, 0.0064, 0.0016, 0.0031, 0.0063, 0.01, 0.0025, 0.005, 0.01, 0.016, 0.0039, 0.0079, 0.0158, 0.0252\nq2: 0.0032, 0.0064, 0.0102, 0.0025, 0.005, 0.01, 0.016, 0.004, 0.008, 0.016, 0.0256, 0.0063, 0.0126, 0.0252, 0.0403\nq3: 1.6, 1.6, 1.6, 2.5, 2.5, 2.5, 2.5, 4.0, 4.0, 4.0, 4.0, 6.3, 6.3, 6.3, 6.3\nq4: 2.0, 2.0, 2.0, 3.13, 3.13, 3.13, 3.13, 5.0, 5.0, 5.0, 5.0, 7.88, 7.88, 7.88, 7.88\nq3_q1_ratio: 800, 400, 250, 1600, 800, 400, 250, 1600, 800, 400, 250, 1600, 800, 400, 250\n\nAttribute names: \"q1\"..\"q4\", \"q3_q1_ratio\", \"nominal_diameter\".\n\nMaybe also include per-model attributes in models[]? I'll keep models[] minimal with model_id and label.\n\nLabel: e.g. \"WMP DN15, Q3 = 1.6 m³/h, R = 800\". I'll generate for each.\n\nActually, maybe skip labels to reduce bulk? Schema allows label optional. I'll include short labels since helpful. Eh, 15 labels — fine.\n\nNow certified_type.description: compose: \"Ultrasonic water meter (R49) with non-separable case; access to the metering part only by visibly damaging the case. Optional sealing by wire with seal mark; optional two security seals (wire holes) or two self-destructive stickers protecting the power source; detachable blue protective cap. Wireless communication (BLE, WMbus, LoRa) and starting flow (0.001 m³/h) are manufacturer information and not certified.\"\n\nAlso mention accuracy class 2, E1 M1 O classes? Those are in type_level. Description keep as above.\n\nissuing_authority: name only. person_responsible etc not present → omit. Should I include oiml_issuer_id \"CZ1\"? It's derived from cert number substring. In OIML CS, the number format is R49/2013-A-CZ1-26.03 where CZ1 is the issuer. I'm fairly confident. Include.\n\nmember_state: \"Czech Republic\" — inference from ČMI. I'll include in certificate.member_state? Hmm. ČMI is the Czech NMI; member state Czech Republic. It's mild inference; I'll include \"Czech Republic\"? The rule: don't fabricate. The stamp says \"Český metrologický institut (Czech Metrology Institute)\". Member state Czechia. I'll include it — reasonable extraction. Hmm, but cautious: I'll include member_state: \"Czech Republic\". OK.\n\nscheme: \"A\" from number. Include.\n\nproject_number: not present → omit. date_issued: not present in fragment → omit.\n\nAlso should I include \"test_reports\"? Not present. \"revision_history\"? Not present. Footnotes: none numbered. Omit footnotes.\n\nPage 9 dial: \"Battery expiry date (Replace by 2042, Li)\" — example-unit specific; skip. Serial number example; skip.\n\nNow write final JSON. Careful with escaping the inch quote in \"G 3/4\\\"\".\n\nFor orientation value, avoid ↓ maybe: \"vertical, flow from top to bottom (V↓)\" — JSON supports unicode. Fine.\n\nLet me also double-check \"Accuracy class: 2\" — water meter accuracy class 2. Type-level accuracy_class value 2, no unit.\n\n\"Temperature class: T30 or T50\" — string.\n\nOne more: \"Q₃ flowrates: 1.6 / 2.5 / 4.0 / 6.3 m³/h\" page 5 — consistent with tables; covered by model_level.\n\n\"Nominal diameters: 15, 20, 25 mm\" — covered.\n\nAlso \"Manufacturers\" — page 5 \"Type Details: Manufacturer: ADD Production\". Also page 9 \"manufacturer: ADDRA (trademark)\". Should manufacturers[] include ADDRA? ADDRA is the trademark/brand of ADD Production (Made in Moldova). The dial legend: \"Meter manufacturer trademark (ADDRA)\". I'll include manufacturers: [{\"name\": \"ADD Production\"}] only; mention ADDRA in description? Eh, add to description: \"trademark ADDRA\". Sure, minor.\n\nHmm, actually is ADD Production the manufacturer and applicant? Fragment doesn't say applicant. Only manufacturers.\n\nOrder of model ids — I should double check uniqueness and clarity. \"Q3=2.5_R800_DN15/20\" vs \"Q3=2.5_R800_DN15\" — the latter doesn't exist (DN15 R800 is Q3=1.6). Wait: Table 1 col 5-7 are \"15 and 20\" with Q3=2.5, R 800/400/250. And col 1-3 DN15 Q3=1.6 R 800/400/250. So \"DN15_R800\" ambiguous — but my ids include Q3, so unique. Good.\n\nAlternatively simpler ids: \"T1-C1\"..\"T2-C7\"? Less meaningful. Keep metrological ids.\n\nLet me write label format: \"WMP DN15, Q3 = 1.6 m³/h, R800\". For dual: \"WMP DN15/20, Q3 = 2.5 m³/h, R800\".\n\nFinal check on unit symbols: m³/h — superscript ³ as unicode \"m³/h\". °C, MPa, V, mm, m³, years.\n\nminimum_battery_life unit: \"years\" — maybe \"a\". I'll use \"years\".\n\nNow model_level array entries format:\n\n{\"attribute\": \"nominal_diameter\", \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"...\", \"value\": \"15\"}, ...]}\n{\"attribute\": \"q1\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"...\", \"value\": 0.002}, ...]}\n...\n\nAlso include \"unit_id\": null at attribute level? The R137 example didn't include unit_id. Skip.\n\nmatrix_tables name: \"Basic metrological data (flowrates) — Table 1\" and \"... Table 2\". Description optional: mention that columns are meter variants; last column of Table 2 empty and omitted. I'll add description: \"Columns of the source table are meter variants; empty eighth column of Table 2 omitted.\" And footnotes: [] omit.\n\nAlso connection sizes: source \"G3 / 4\\\", G1, G1 / 1/4\\\"\" — OCR garbled: likely G 3/4\", G 1\", G 1¼\". I'll write \"G 3/4\\\", G 1, G 1 1/4\\\"\". Fine.\n\nLength ranges: \"110–165 / 130–195 / 190–225\" with unit mm. Use en-dash? Use plain hyphen \"110-165 / 130-195 / 190-225\". I'll use \"110–165 / 130–195 / 190–225\" (en dashes fine in JSON). I'll keep simple hyphens to be safe: \"110-165 / 130-195 / 190-225\".\n\nEnvironment temp: -25 to +70 → {\"min\": -25, \"max\": 70}, unit °C.\n\nNow, \"installed_length_brass_body\" — the page 6 table: \"Length [mm] – brass body\". Label: \"length_brass_body\". I'll use \"length_brass_body\".\n\nAssemble now. Also include \"components\"? The sealing elements: optional security seals, stickers, protective cap — these are optional accessories, not sub-assemblies. Skip components.\n\nDouble-check numeric: indicating range 99 999 → 99999. Resolution 0.000 001 → 0.000001 (1e-6).\n\nWrite the JSON.\n\nOne consideration: certificate.oiml_issuer_id — \"CZ1\". And issuing_authority.oiml_issuer_id same. OK.\n\nrecommendation.accuracy_classes: [\"2\"]. Hmm also class could be \"2\" only. Yes.\n\nLet me also reconsider whether to include type_level \"eut_category\" and \"eut_test_case\" — they're present. Keep, labels: \"eut_category\" and \"eut_test_case\". Values \"Ultrasonic water meter\" and \"B\". Also note reference R49-2:2013 §8.1.8 — could add footnote? Skip.\n\nAlso \"Software versions: V80, V81\" plus CRC \"5A3210F1 / 9d3A5b4d\" — two CRCs correspond to two versions presumably, but pairing not explicit (order implies V80→5A3210F1, V81→9d3A5b4d). Keep as one string each.\n\nFinal JSON size manageable. Write it.",
